      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 18,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## How long can the video be? (`length` on this node)\n\n`length` must be **4n+1** (81, 85, 89 … 121). Duration = length ÷ 16 fps.\n\n| length | duration | expect |\n|---|---|---|\n| **81 (shipped)** | **5.0 s** | Wan 2.2's training length, most reliable |\n| 97–121 | 6–7.5 s | usually fine; motion may slow or repeat at the end |\n| 161+ | 10 s+ | runs, but loops back, the face drifts, colours shift, end freezes or falls apart |\n\n**Cost:** the model handles all frames together, so cost grows faster than length. At 832×624, 81 frames took about 15–18 s per step; 121 frames should be about 2.2× per step (estimate). 32 GB handles up to about 160 frames.\n\n**Longer than about 10 s: render in parts, don't raise `length`**\n1. `Wan 2.2 Video Infinity.json` (SVI 2 PRO LoRAs, on the host). Each part is 81 frames and starts from the end of the previous one, with blended overlap and its own prompt. Effectively unlimited; drift is the limit.\n2. Simple chaining: use the last frame as the next start frame. Small changes in the character add up, and motion can jump at each join.\n\n**Smoother, not longer:** RIFE interpolation (`comfyui-frame-interpolation`) turns 16 fps into 32 fps. Play at 32 fps for smoother motion, or at 16 fps for a 10 s slow-motion version of the same 5 s.\n\n**Suggested:** try 113–121 frames here for a 7 s shot. Use an SVI extend stage for more than 10 s.\n"
      ],
      "title": "VIDEO LENGTH — read before changing length",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
